 myself why  do I do that for helping my kid with  their homework because I'd be so  embarrassed if my kid was revealed as  being a dummy who can't do basic  mathematics right and I'm invested  myself or I'm trying to get them ready  for the academic quiz bowl that I'm  pushing them towards so I can bask in  the glory of having a brilliant child or  whatever else that's gonna be trying to  keep the teachers off my kids back and  therefore off my back those are  self-interested motives and so Peter  Singer says that the practice of  reciprocal altruism that it should be  the source of many of our attitudes of  moral approval and disapproval including  ideas of fairness cheating gratitude and  retribution would be easier to accept if  it were not that this explanation seems  to put these attitudes and ideas on too  self-centered of a footing so he says  reciprocal altruism seems as if it's not  really altruism at all but a concealed  form of egoism a concealed form of  self-centered decision-making saying  what am I going to get out of it but I'm  not getting something out of it then I'm  not going to contribute now why would  reciprocal altruism look that way and  altruism per se not look that way  because reciprocal altruism says you do  good things for the person who also  benefits you right you don't you don't  benefit everybody indiscriminately that  would in fact be imprudent or foolish  and lead to you being shortchanged and  likewise reciprocal altruism would say  not only the ones who cheat or don't  reciprocate  can you feel certain negative feelings  towards them like indignation or  annoyance but you might actually even  take revenge on them you might punish  them for not reciprocating so that seems  rather self-interest that he says it  might really just be accurately  described  enlightened self-interest and this is  this is a true thing in many cases there  are a lot of people who are quite frank  and upfront about the fact that they're  only gonna do stuff for you because  they're getting something out of it so  he says one might be a fully  reciprocating partner in this practice  without having the slightest concern for  the welfare of the person one helps  concern for one's own interests plus the  knowledge that exchanges of assistance  are likely to be in the long-term  interests of both partners that's all  that's needed he says now consider an  example and see how this sits with you  so we've got again you know saving  somebody who is is having a very rough  time by drowning he says if I'm drowning  in a raging surf and a stranger plunges  in and rescue rescues me I shall be very  grateful and you know we show this in  all sorts of ways what if we find out he  says that the stranger recognised me as  being a person of means and then did a  mental calculation and said it's going  to be profitable to rescue this person  they will probably give me a nice reward  and somebody else is drowning further  down the beach and they're like well  that person's retirement portfolio is  not quite so good I'm gonna let them  drown well we would feel a sense of  something being wrong there right we  would say that that's the wrong  motivation to have and we wouldn't see  that person as being genuinely  altruistic and he says not it's not only  gratitude that diminishes when  self-interested motives are revealed  moral approval is always warmest for  acts which show either spontaneous  concern for the welfare of others or  else a conscientious desire to do what  is right proof that in action we have  praised that a self-interested motive  almost always leads us to withdraw or to  qualify to lessen our praise so singer  says well maybe we went wrong in how we  to find altruism so he says earlier in  this chapter here's the definition that  we accepted in terms of behavior  altruistic behavior is behavior which  benefits others at some cost to oneself  now notice that that has nothing to say  about intention or motivation why 