r people or damage their cars or you  know create dangerous situations so  motivation would would matter then and  he says that let's let's think about how  this would would play itself out in  terms of an evolutionary schema or  explanation says Robert Trivers has  offered a sociobiological explanation  for our moral preference for altruistic  motivation why is it that we prefer  other people to be altruistic one reason  is very simple almost common sensical is  that people who are altruistically  motivated make more reliable partners in  whatever exchanges or other relations  that were involved in more reliable than  those motivated by self-interest how so  well you know you can say if they really  care at least some amount about how  other people are going to be affected  positively or negatively they will do  the right thing even when nobody's  watching  even when it's going to cut into their  profits a little bit more even when it  might be a little bit more  psychologically difficult so they can be  relied upon and he says after all one  day the calculations of self-interest  may turn out differently and he's got  the goes back to the beach example says  looking at the shabby clothes I have  left on the beach is self-interested  potential rescuer may decide that the  prospect of a sizable reward are dim so  we we want people who will rescue us or  do the other things that we want them to  do in similar circumstances regardless  of whether they're going to get  something out of it directly  so he says evolution would therefore  favor people who could distinguish  self-interested from altruistic  motivation and others then select only  the altruistic as beneficiaries other  gifts and services so he's talking about  how reciprocal altruism would function  well if evolution whether it's  understood and purely genetic terms are  also in terms of its interaction with  culture if if these processes would lead  to some things being favoured and other  things being dis